window.docContents[112].resolve({"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4-using-Hom-decomposition/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4-using-Hom-decomposition--Alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4-using-Hom-decomposition--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For semisimple modules whose simple submodules come from a fixed pairwise nonisomorphic family, a linear map is bijective exactly when all induced maps on the corresponding Hom spaces are bijective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3.1.4; role=primary]\n\nThe linear equivalence from maps between semisimple modules to families of postcomposition maps between their Hom spaces from representative simple modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3.1.4; role=primary]\n\nThe component of the Hom-space equivalence sends a map from a representative simple module to its composite with the original module map.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3.1.4; role=primary]\n\nThe multiplicity map of a composite is the pointwise composite of the corresponding multiplicity maps.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3.1.4; role=primary]\n\nThe multiplicity map of the identity is the pointwise identity family.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3.1.4; role=primary]\n\nFor semisimple modules with the displayed complete simple family, a linear map is injective exactly when all induced maps on the corresponding Hom spaces are injective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3.1.4; role=primary]\n\nFor semisimple modules with the displayed complete simple family, a linear map is surjective exactly when all induced maps on the corresponding Hom spaces are surjective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3.1.4; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Alternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4 using Hom decomposition\u0009Alternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4 using Hom decomposition\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4-using-Hom-decomposition/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4-using-Hom-decomposition--Alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4-using-Hom-decomposition--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___2___-The-density-theorem/Any-linear-map-on-linearly-independent-vectors-is-realized-by-an-algebra-element/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___2___-The-density-theorem--Any-linear-map-on-linearly-independent-vectors-is-realized-by-an-algebra-element--Any-linear-map-on-linearly-independent-vectors-is-realized-by-an-algebra-element--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"On a simple finite-dimensional module over an algebraically closed field, any prescribed images of a finite linearly independent family are simultaneously realized by one algebra element.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Corollary3.2.1; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Introduction_to_3.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2/Derived2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.2. The density theorem\u0009Any linear map on linearly independent vectors is realized by an algebra element\u0009Any linear map on linearly independent vectors is realized by an algebra element\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___2___-The-density-theorem/Any-linear-map-on-linearly-independent-vectors-is-realized-by-an-algebra-element/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___2___-The-density-theorem--Any-linear-map-on-linearly-independent-vectors-is-realized-by-an-algebra-element--Any-linear-map-on-linearly-independent-vectors-is-realized-by-an-algebra-element--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Idempotents-from-characters-in-the-group-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters--Idempotents-from-characters-in-the-group-algebra--Idempotents-from-characters-in-the-group-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary element of the complex group algebra associated to a finite-dimensional complex representation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Problem4.5.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe product of the auxiliary group-algebra elements of two nonisomorphic simple representations is zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Problem4.5.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary group-algebra element associated to a simple representation is idempotent.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Problem4.5.2; role=supporting]\n\nA simple representation sends its associated auxiliary group-algebra element to the identity linear map.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Problem4.5.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe algebra action of one simple representation sends the auxiliary element of a nonisomorphic simple representation to zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Problem4.5.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.5. Orthogonality of characters\u0009Idempotents from characters in the group algebra\u0009Idempotents from characters in the group algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Idempotents-from-characters-in-the-group-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters--Idempotents-from-characters-in-the-group-algebra--Idempotents-from-characters-in-the-group-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Types-of-irreducible-representations-of-Z___nZ___-S_3___-S_4___-A_5___-Q_8/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator--Types-of-irreducible-representations-of-Z___nZ___-S_3___-S_4___-A_5___-Q_8--Types-of-irreducible-representations-of-Z___nZ___-S_3___-S_4___-A_5___-Q_8--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary condition on the value constructed from a cyclic character holds exactly when the character is nontrivial and differs from the specified character for every proof that two divides the order.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary condition on the value constructed from a cyclic character holds exactly when the character is trivial or agrees with the specified character for some proof that two divides the order.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\nEvery complex representation of the quaternion group on the complex numbers has the auxiliary property.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\nA simple finite-dimensional complex representation of the alternating group on five letters has the auxiliary property.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\nA simple finite-dimensional complex representation of the symmetric group on four letters has the auxiliary property.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\nA simple finite-dimensional complex representation of the symmetric group on three letters has the auxiliary property.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\nThere exists a simple complex representation of the quaternion group satisfying the auxiliary property.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\nA simple complex representation of a finite cyclic group of positive order is isomorphic to one of the auxiliary representations.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\nA simple two-dimensional complex representation of the quaternion group is isomorphic to the specified representation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.1.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.1. Frobenius-Schur indicator\u0009Types of irreducible representations of Z/nZ, S_3, S_4, A_5, Q_8\u0009Types of irreducible representations of Z/nZ, S_3, S_4, A_5, Q_8\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Types-of-irreducible-representations-of-Z___nZ___-S_3___-S_4___-A_5___-Q_8/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator--Types-of-irreducible-representations-of-Z___nZ___-S_3___-S_4___-A_5___-Q_8--Types-of-irreducible-representations-of-Z___nZ___-S_3___-S_4___-A_5___-Q_8--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Section-5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.2. Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers\u0009Section 5.2: Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers\u0009Section 5.2: Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Section-5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___2___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories/Examples-of-equivalent-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories--Examples-of-equivalent-categories--Examples-of-equivalent-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: FintypeCat.Skeleton.equivalence\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Definition7.4.1; role=supporting\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.4. Equivalence of categories\u0009Examples of equivalent categories\u0009Examples of equivalent categories\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories/Examples-of-equivalent-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories--Examples-of-equivalent-categories--Examples-of-equivalent-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Equivalent-characterizations-of-injective-modules/#Chapter8___Theorem8___1___5":{"contents":"Theorem 8.1.5. The following properties of I are equivalent:\n\n(i) If \\alpha : N \\to M is an injective morphism and \\nu : N \\to I is any morphism, then there exists a morphism \\mu : M \\to I such that \\mu \\circ \\alpha = \\nu.\n\n(ii) Any injective morphism \\alpha : I \\to M splits; i.e., there exists \\mu : M \\to I such that \\mu \\circ \\alpha = \\operatorname{id}.\n(iii) The functor \\operatorname{Hom}_A(?, I) on the category of A-modules is exact.\n\nProof. The proof of the implications \"(i) implies (ii)\" and \"(iii) implies (i)\" is similar to the proof of Theorem 8.1.1. Let us prove that (ii) implies (iii). Let\n\n\n0 \\to N \\to M \\to K \\to 0\n\n\nbe an exact sequence. Denote the embedding N \\to M by j. Consider the corresponding sequence\n\n\n0 \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(K, I) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(M, I) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(N, I) \\to 0.\n\n\nLet f \\in \\operatorname{Hom}(N, I), and define the module E := (M \\oplus I)/N, where N is embedded into M \\oplus I via x \\mapsto (j(x), -f(x)). Clearly, we have an inclusion I \\to E, since the image of N \\oplus I in E is naturally identified with I. So there is a splitting E \\to I of this inclusion, i.e., a map M \\oplus I \\to I, (m, i) \\mapsto g(m) + i such that g(j(x)) - f(x) = 0. This means that the map j^* : \\operatorname{Hom}(M, I) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(N, I) is surjective, i.e., the functor \\operatorname{Hom}(?, I) is exact, as desired. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules\u0009Equivalent characterizations of injective modules","header":"Equivalent characterizations of injective modules","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Equivalent-characterizations-of-injective-modules/#Chapter8___Theorem8___1___5"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Chapter9___Definition9___1___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.1. Lifting of idempotents\u0009Complete system of orthogonal idempotents\u0009Complete system of orthogonal idempotents","header":"Formalization","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Chapter9___Definition9___1___2___formalization"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Basic-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Basic-algebra--Basic-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The property holds for a commutative ring carrying an algebra structure over a field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.7.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.7. Morita equivalence\u0009Basic algebra\u0009Basic algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Basic-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Basic-algebra--Basic-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});